# PHASE 06 — COMMON QR VERIFICATION & LUCKY DRAW ENGINE

## Objective

Build the complete wedding venue verification and lucky draw system.

This phase must use the participant records created in Phase 05.

The QR workflow is already approved by the project owner.

**One common event QR code. Registered mobile number matching. Automatic verification. No OTP.**

## 6.1 Common event QR code

Create one common event QR code.

The QR must contain a verification URL such as:

```text
https://your-domain.com/verify
```

Do not create individual QR codes for participants.

Do not encode participant personal information into the QR code.

The same QR code must work for all registered guests.

Provide the administrator with the ability to:

- View the QR code.
- Download as PNG.
- Generate a print-ready PDF.
- Activate or deactivate the verification endpoint.

The QR should be suitable for printing and displaying at the wedding venue.

## 6.2 Guest verification page

Build a simple, mobile-first page.

Page title:

**Wedding Guest Verification**

Supporting text:

Scan the event QR code and enter your registered mobile number to confirm your participation.

Input:

- Registered mobile number.

Primary button:

**Verify My Registration**

Do not request:

- OTP.
- Email verification.
- Password.
- Individual QR code.
- Additional identity documents.

## 6.3 Verification backend

Implement the verification flow securely.

1. Guest scans the common QR.
2. Website opens the verification page.
3. Guest enters their registered mobile number.
4. Frontend submits the number to a server-side endpoint.
5. Backend normalises the number.
6. Backend looks up the matching participant.
7. Backend checks that registration is eligible.
8. Backend checks whether the participant is already verified.
9. Backend atomically marks the participant verified.
10. Backend records the verification timestamp and check-in record.
11. Frontend displays the result.

Never perform privileged verification updates directly from an untrusted browser client.

## 6.4 Verification states

Handle these situations:

| Condition | Response |
|---|---|
| Valid pending participant | Verification successful |
| Already verified | Already verified |
| Mobile number not found | Registration not found |
| Registration closed | Registration is closed |
| Verification disabled | Verification currently unavailable |
| Invalid input | Enter a valid mobile number |
| Server error | Unable to verify right now |

Do not reveal whether an unrelated guest's email or other private information exists.

## 6.5 Prevent duplicate check-in

A participant must have only one successful verification.

Use a database transaction or atomic conditional update.

Use a unique constraint on the participant's check-in record.

Concurrent requests must not create multiple successful check-ins.

Create a check-in record containing:

```text
id
participant_id
verification_method
verified_at
created_at
```

The verification method can be recorded as:

```text
common_qr_mobile_match
```

Do not create OTP fields or OTP-related infrastructure.

## 6.6 Verification success screen

Show:

**Successfully Verified!**

Your wedding activity registration has been confirmed.

You are eligible to participate in the wedding lucky draw, subject to the final draw rules.

Show the participant's name and side only where appropriate.

Keep the page elegant, clear and mobile-friendly.

## 6.7 Verification security limitation

This workflow intentionally uses mobile-number matching without OTP.

A person who knows another guest's registered mobile number could potentially verify that participant.

This trade-off is accepted by the project owner.

Implement reasonable abuse controls:

- Rate limit verification attempts.
- Avoid exposing participant records.
- Use generic failure responses.
- Record successful check-ins.
- Log suspicious repeated attempts.
- Protect admin-only verification controls.

Do not silently add OTP or change the approved workflow.

## 6.8 Admin verification data

Build the services required for the admin interface.

Provide:

- Total registrations.
- Total verified.
- Total pending.
- Bride Side verified.
- Groom Side verified.
- Participant check-in timestamps.
- Manual verification capability for authorised admins.

Manual verification must record:

- Admin ID.
- Participant ID.
- Timestamp.
- Reason.
- Previous status.
- New status.

## 6.9 Lucky draw eligibility

Default eligibility:

**Only successfully verified participants.**

The draw must use the combined Bride Side and Groom Side verified participant pool.

Default winner count:

**11 winners in total.**

Not 11 winners per side.

Provide configuration for:

- Number of winners.
- Eligible participant pool.
- Draw status.
- Registration status.
- Verification status.

The default eligibility setting must remain verified participants only.

If an admin is allowed to select all registered participants instead, this must be an explicit authorised configuration, not an accidental default.

## 6.10 Lucky draw execution

The draw must execute securely on the server.

Requirements:

- Use a cryptographically secure random generator.
- Never use browser Math.random() as the winner-selection mechanism.
- Select unique participant IDs.
- Prevent duplicate winners.
- Validate the eligible participant count.
- Prevent execution when there are insufficient eligible participants.
- Save the result permanently.
- Prevent accidental reruns.

Create an immutable draw execution record containing:

```text
draw_id
event_id
draw_status
winner_count
eligibility_rule
eligible_participant_snapshot
executed_by
executed_at
```

Store the selected winners in a separate winner table.

The draw should execute atomically, so a failed or interrupted process cannot produce an incomplete public result.

Use an appropriate transaction or secure server-side execution strategy.

## 6.11 Winner records

Each winner record should contain:

```text
id
draw_id
participant_id
winner_position
gift_status
created_at
updated_at
```

Gift statuses:

- Pending.
- Contacted.
- Delivered.

Enforce uniqueness so a participant cannot win twice in the same draw.

## 6.12 Public winner announcement

Create:

```text
/lucky-draw/winners
```

Display:

- Winner position.
- Approved public display name.
- Bride Side / Groom Side.
- Announcement date.

Do not expose:

- Mobile number.
- Email address.
- Full residential address.
- Private participant records.

Provide a graceful state before the draw:

**The lucky draw winners will be announced soon.**

Do not expose unfinished draw records.

